# The national unit for russia. Items take n=3; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g4-russia-pages-of-our-history v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=9 subject=history nation=russia levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Pages of Russia's history"
  goal "Put pages of Russia's history in order, count the years between them, and say why Peter the Great built a city by the sea."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=900 to=1800 step=150 years=[988, 1147, 1703] labels=["Rus baptised", "Moscow first mentioned", "St Petersburg founded"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      About 988 Prince Vladimir of Kyiv was baptised, and with him Rus. Moscow is first named in the chronicle in 1147. On 27 May 1703 Tsar Peter the Great founded St Petersburg on the Baltic Sea, and it became the capital. In 1755 Russia's first university opened in Moscow, which the scientist Mikhail Lomonosov had argued for.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n4-what n=3
    }
    show history.n4-order n=3 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n4-between n=3 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n4-century n=3 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.source-hand k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.bc-ad-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.money-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n4-order n=3 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n4-what n=3
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n4-century n=3 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.source-hand k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n4-between n=3 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n4-why n=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n4-find-out n=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Every date in our country's story can be put on the line and counted from, and every one came from a source."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to use the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, sources) on a
    chapter of the family's own country. The way in names what a thing was; the core orders three
    events, counts the years between two, names a century, and reviews sources, BC and AD and the
    story of money; the two-star question asks why something was done. If the child is stuck on
    counting, count to the next hundred first. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you. Russia's «Окружающий мир» for grade four includes pages of the country's history (recalled from the federal programme, not quoted here), and grade six history begins «От Руси к Российскому государству».
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the thing was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions are the plainest."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
